{"id":"1cb9487a-05da-463c-8043-a9797af7c9fc","object":"chat.completion","created":1785021554,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    for key in ['passing', 'failing', 'required_difference', 'unresolved_difference']:\\n        if key not in case:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Missing field: {key}')\\n    passing_set = set(case['passing'])\\n    diff = [x for x in case['failing'] if x not in passing_set]\\n    required = case['required_difference']\\n    unresolved = case['unresolved_difference']\\n    if not all(x in diff for x in required):\\n        raise ValueError('required_difference not subset of difference')\\n    if not all(x in diff for x in unresolved):\\n        raise ValueError('unresolved_difference not subset of difference')\\n\\n    tests = 0\\n\\n    def oracle(subset):\\n        nonlocal tests\\n        tests += 1\\n        if all(x in subset for x in required):\\n            return 'FAIL'\\n        if any(x in subset for x in unresolved):\\n            return 'UNRESOLVED'\\n        return 'PASS'\\n\\n    if not diff:\\n        return {'isolated_difference': [], 'tests': tests}\\n\\n    changes = list(diff)\\n    n = 2\\n    while len(changes) > 1:\\n        # partition changes into n subsets as equally as possible\\n        subset_sizes = [len(changes) // n] * n\\n        for i in range(len(changes) % n):\\n            subset_sizes[i] += 1\\n        subsets = []\\n        idx = 0\\n        for size in subset_sizes:\\n            subsets.append(changes[idx:idx + size])\\n            idx += size\\n\\n        found = False\\n        for subset in subsets:\\n            if oracle(subset) == 'FAIL':\\n                changes = subset\\n                n = 2\\n                found = True\\n                break\\n        if found:\\n            continue\\n\\n        for subset in subsets:\\n            complement = [x for x in changes if x not in subset]\\n            if not complement:\\n                continue\\n            if oracle(complement) == 'FAIL':\\n                changes = complement\\n                n = 2\\n                found = True\\n                break\\n        if found:\\n            continue\\n\\n        if n >= len(changes):\\n            break\\n        n = min(2 * n, len(changes))\\n\\n    return {'isolated_difference': changes, 'tests': tests}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-DD-02. The task is about \"difference isolation\" using delta debugging (ddmin). The case payload contains: passing (list of strings), failing (list of strings), required_difference (list of strings), unresolved_difference (list of strings), and _case_nonce (string). The goal is to isolate a minimal causal difference between passing and failing configurations.\n\nFrom the problem description: \"Compute the ordered change set present in failing but absent from passing.\" So we need to find elements that are in failing but not in passing. Then run ddmin only on this difference. ddmin is a delta debugging algorithm that minimizes the failing input. We need to return \"isolated_difference\" (list of strings) and \"tests\" (integer number of tests performed). The output schema requires exactly those two keys.\n\nWe need to implement ddmin. However, note that the input has \"passing\" and \"failing\" which are lists of identifiers. In the delta debugging context, the \"failing\" list is the set of changes that cause failure, but here we are given two lists: passing (passing configuration) and failing (failing configuration). The difference is the set of elements that are in failing but not in passing (ordered? As per \"ordered change set\"). Then we need to run ddmin on that difference to find a minimal subset that still causes failure.\n\nBut what does \"failure\" mean? We have no oracle function; we need to simulate the oracle based on the given data. The payload has \"required_difference\" and \"unresolved_difference\". For a given subset (candidate difference), we need to evaluate whether it passes or fails. How? The problem says \"Preserve the registered PASS/FAIL/UNRESOLVED oracle semantics.\" The oracle should be based on comparing the candidate subset against the required_difference and unresolved_difference. The required_difference are elements that must be present for failure to occur? Actually, typical delta debugging: we have a test that returns PASS or FAIL. Here we are given a list of changes (elements) that are present in failing but not in passing. The failing list is the full set of changes. The required_difference might be the minimal set that causes failure? In the public fixtures, required_difference is always a subset of the failing-passing difference. For example, in first case: failing minus passing = [x0, x1, x2, x3, x4, x5]. required_difference = [x4, x5]. So the oracle: a candidate subset of the difference is \"failing\" if it contains all elements of required_difference? That seems plausible. But \"unresolved_difference\" is empty in these cases. Perhaps the oracle is: given a subset S, if S contains all elements of required_difference, then it's FAIL? And if it contains none or some but not all? Also need to consider unresolved_difference: maybe those are elements that if present cause unresolved? The problem mentions \"UNRESOLVED oracle semantics\". In the paper, the oracle can return PASS, FAIL, or UNRESOLVED. So we need to implement a function that given a subset S returns one of those based on the payload's fields.\n\nLet's look at the payload structure: passing and failing are lists. required_difference and unresolved_difference are lists. They likely encode the minimal failing set and some unresolved set. Perhaps the oracle rules:\n- A test case is a subset S of the difference set (i.e., elements that are in failing but not in passing).\n- If S contains all elements of required_difference, then the test fails (FAIL).\n- If S contains any element of unresolved_difference, then the test is UNRESOLVED.\n- Otherwise, the test passes (PASS).\n\nBut we need to confirm with the fixtures. For case 0: required_difference = [x4, x5]; unresolved_difference = [].\nIf we take S = [x4, x5], then it contains required_difference, so FAIL.\nIf we take S = [x4], does it contain all of required? No, so it would be PASS. That seems plausible: only the full set causes failure.\nFor case 1: required = [x2, x4]; so S = [x2, x4] is FAIL.\nFor case 2: required = [x2, x5]; S = [x2, x5] is FAIL.\n\nThus the ddmin should find the minimal subset that still fails, which should be exactly required_difference (since that's given as minimal). So the algorithm should return required_difference as isolated_difference, and tests count.\n\nBut need to run ddmin. The standard ddmin algorithm minimizes a set by testing subsets. We need to implement it exactly as per the delta debugging paper (Zeller). The algorithm takes a set of changes (the failing set) and returns a minimal failing subset. We need to count the number of tests performed.\n\nWe'll implement solve(case):\n1. Validate case has required fields: passing, failing, required_difference, unresolved_difference. Possibly ignore _case_nonce.\n2. Compute the difference: set of elements in failing not in passing. But keep order? The problem says \"ordered change set present in failing but absent from passing.\" So the difference should be the list of elements in failing that are not in passing, preserving the order they appear in failing. So we can iterate over failing and include if not in passing (as set for efficiency).\n3. Define oracle function: given a subset (list) of changes, check if it contains all elements of required_difference; if yes -> FAIL, else if it contains any element of unresolved_difference -> UNRESOLVED, else -> PASS. But what about the case where a subset contains both required and unresolved? It should be FAIL? The problem likely defines FAIL as containing all required; UNRESOLVED if contains any unresolved but not all required. So order: first check required, then unresolved.\n4. Run ddmin on the full difference list (ordered). The ddmin algorithm from paper: \n   function ddmin(c):\n       if len(c) == 1: return c\n       n = 2\n       while len(c) > 1:\n           # partition into n subsets\n           # test each subset and its complement? Actually ddmin tests subsets and their complements. The standard ddmin tests each subset of size len(c)/n, and if any fails, set c to that subset and continue with n=2; else if any complement fails, set c to complement and continue with n=2; else increase n unless n > len(c).\n       Might need to implement exactly as per paper's pseudocode. We'll use the well-known algorithm.\n   But we need to handle UNRESOLVED: in ddmin, if a subset is UNRESOLVED, we treat it as PASS generally? The paper says if unresolved, we consider it as not failing and proceed. So we can treat UNRESOLVED as not FAIL (i.e., continue).\n5. Return {'isolated_difference': final minimized list, 'tests': count of oracle calls made during ddmin}.\n\nWe must count tests. In the algorithm, each call to oracle (test) increments count.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Use Python's standard library only.\n- Do not mutate input.\n- Return JSON-serializable dict.\n\nEdge cases: difference may be empty? Then isolated_difference = [] and tests = 0? But required_difference must be subset of difference, so if difference empty, required must be empty. But if required_difference not empty and not subset, it's invalid? Raise ValueError.\n\nWe'll implement solve(case) that returns the required dict.\n\nNow write the code.\n\nNote: output must be exactly JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the source code as a string.\n\nWe'll produce the JSON.\n\nLet's write the Python function.\n\nWe'll include a nested oracle function that uses the case payload's required_difference and unresolved_difference.\n\nWe'll implement ddmin as a recursive or iterative function.\n\nI'll use iterative ddmin from the paper:\n\ndef ddmin(changes, oracle):\n    n = 2\n    while len(changes) > 1:\n        subsets = partition(changes, n)\n        found = False\n        for subset in subsets:\n            if oracle(subset) == 'FAIL':\n                changes = subset\n                n = 2\n                found = True\n                break\n        if found:\n            continue\n        # test complements\n        for subset in subsets:\n            complement = [x for x in changes if x not in subset]\n            # ensure complement not empty? In standard ddmin, complement could be empty but then test passes? We'll handle.\n            if oracle(complement) == 'FAIL':\n                changes = complement\n                n = 2\n                found = True\n                break\n        if not found:\n            if n >= len(changes):\n                break\n            n = min(2*n, len(changes))\n    return changes\n\nBut we need to maintain order. Partition should preserve order.\n\nWe'll implement partition as splitting into n contiguous chunks.\n\nAlso, we need to count tests. So oracle function will increment a counter.\n\nWe'll use a nonlocal variable for test count.\n\nImplement solve(case):\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # validate fields\n    for key in ['passing', 'failing', 'required_difference', 'unresolved_difference']:\n        if key not in case:\n            raise ValueError(f'Missing field: {key}')\n    passing_set = set(case['passing'])\n    diff = [x for x in case['failing'] if x not in passing_set]\n    required = case['required_difference']\n    unresolved = case['unresolved_difference']\n    # validate that required is subset of diff? Not necessarily? But it should be. If not, raise ValueError.\n    if not all(x in diff for x in required):\n        raise ValueError('required_difference not subset of difference')\n    # check unresolved also subset\n    if not all(x in diff for x in unresolved):\n        raise ValueError('unresolved_difference not subset of difference')\n\n    tests = 0\n    def oracle(subset):\n        nonlocal tests\n        tests += 1\n        # check for required\n        if all(x in subset for x in required):\n            return 'FAIL'\n        # check for unresolved\n        if any(x in subset for x in unresolved):\n            return 'UNRESOLVED'\n        return 'PASS'\n\n    # If diff empty, return empty result\n    if not diff:\n        return {'isolated_difference': [], 'tests': tests}\n\n    # Run ddmin\n    # We'll implement iterative ddmin\n    changes = list(diff)  # copy\n    n = 2\n    while len(changes) > 1:\n        subsets = [changes[i:i+len(changes)//n] for i in range(0, len(changes), len(changes)//n)]\n        # adjust for exact division: the last subset may be longer? Standard ddmin partitions into n subsets as equally as possible, but for simplicity we can use floor division. To match paper, we should partition into n subsets with sizes as equal as possible. Use a function to split into n subsets.\n        # We'll implement a helper.\n        # Let's implement partitioning properly.\n        subset_sizes = [len(changes)//n] * n\n        for i in range(len(changes) % n):\n            subset_sizes[i] += 1\n        subsets = []\n        idx = 0\n        for size in subset_sizes:\n            subsets.append(changes[idx:idx+size])\n            idx += size\n        found = False\n        for subset in subsets:\n            res = oracle(subset)\n            if res == 'FAIL':\n                changes = subset\n                n = 2\n                found = True\n                break\n        if found:\n            continue\n        for subset in subsets:\n            complement = [x for x in changes if x not in subset]\n            # complement must not be empty; but if subset is whole, complement empty, then oracle on empty should return PASS? In algorithm, complement is tested only if non-empty? The paper typically tests complement only if it is non-empty. We'll check.\n            if complement:\n                res = oracle(complement)\n                if res == 'FAIL':\n                    changes = complement\n                    n = 2\n                    found = True\n                    break\n        if not found:\n            if n >= len(changes):\n                break\n            n = min(2*n, len(changes))\n    return {'isolated_difference': changes, 'tests': tests}\n\nTest with the given fixtures.\n\nFor case 0: diff = [x0,x1,x2,x3,x4,x5], required = [x4,x5]. ddmin should eventually find [x4,x5]. At each step oracle is called. We'll trust implementation.\n\nWe must ensure that oracle on empty set returns PASS (since no required, no unresolved). That's correct.\n\nNow output.\n\nBut note: The problem says \"Return the isolated difference and number of tests.\" The schema requires \"tests\" as integer.\n\nWe'll write the code.\n\nConsider using a recursive ddmin? Either is fine.\n\nEdge: Need to handle UNRESOLVED properly: if a subset is UNRESOLVED, we should not consider it as failing. So we treat it as not FAIL.\n\nImplementation note: When testing complement, we need to ensure we don't test empty complement. Standard algorithm tests complement only if it is not empty and if subset is not the whole set? Actually the algorithm in paper: For each subset, test the subset; if subset fails, set c to subset and restart. Otherwise, test the complement; if complement fails, set c to complement and restart. So complement is always tested if subset did not fail. But complement could be empty if subset is whole set. That case: subset = full set, complement empty. But we already tested subset (full set) which should fail because it contains required. So we wouldn't get to test complement. So we can guard against empty complement.\n\nWe'll also not test complement if subset is empty? But subset won't be empty in partition.\n\nWe'll implement.\n\nNow produce the JSON.\n\nMake sure to escape newlines etc.\n\nWe'll write the source in a string.\n\nI'll construct the JSON.\n\nImplementation string will contain the solve function.\n\nWe'll include proper indentation.\n\nLet's write the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1278,"completion_tokens":3607,"total_tokens":4885,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1152},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3046},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1152,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":126},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}